WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.761 --> 00:00:15.140
- Did you have any idea how bad
things would get at Memorial?

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:16.558
- Nobody knew.

00:00:19.895 --> 00:00:21.522
- In this particular story,

00:00:21.522 --> 00:00:25.025
the physical space is such
an important character.

00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:28.320
That's why we spent the time
on these sets

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.572
to make them reflect
the actual spaces

00:00:30.572 --> 00:00:32.908
that these people
had to live in.

00:00:32.908 --> 00:00:36.328
What really sets these sets
apart from a lot of other sets

00:00:36.328 --> 00:00:39.540
is the--the sheer scope and size
of them.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:43.001
- Yeah. Wow.
- Yeah.

00:00:43.001 --> 00:00:46.213
- The fidelity to details
is just stunning.

00:00:46.213 --> 00:00:49.383
The exterior, the way
that the windows are broken,

00:00:49.383 --> 00:00:52.469
the--this is just amazing.

00:00:57.891 --> 00:00:59.434
Hurricane Katrina hit,

00:00:59.434 --> 00:01:02.604
and I think it was a shock
to all of us

00:01:02.604 --> 00:01:04.690
who did
international response work,

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:08.193
we kind of assumed that if there
was a big crisis in the U.S.,

00:01:08.193 --> 00:01:12.030
naively, it turns out, that
our infrastructure would hold up

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:16.243
in a better way
than in some countries

00:01:16.243 --> 00:01:19.079
that didn't have as big
of national, you know, GDP

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:22.499
or were seen as low-income
or less resourced,

00:01:22.499 --> 00:01:25.127
and the shock of Katrina
for a lot of Americans

00:01:25.127 --> 00:01:27.713
was that, no, we have
huge vulnerabilities,

00:01:27.713 --> 00:01:29.131
but the vulnerabilities,

00:01:29.131 --> 00:01:32.342
the inability to mount
an effective response

00:01:32.342 --> 00:01:35.888
to a crisis was
pretty important to note,

00:01:35.888 --> 00:01:39.516
and I felt at that point,
I'd worked in foreign disasters

00:01:39.516 --> 00:01:42.978
that it was important to look
at my own country.

00:01:42.978 --> 00:01:44.354
- This building can take
these winds.

00:01:44.354 --> 00:01:46.356
- It's been here 80 years.
It can take it.

00:01:46.356 --> 00:01:49.026
Only thing to worry about,
worst case--severe flood.

00:01:49.026 --> 00:01:50.652
- Okay.

00:01:50.652 --> 00:01:52.654
If there was severe flooding,
I mean, what would we do?

00:01:52.654 --> 00:01:56.700
- We'd have to evacuate.
- Evacuate?

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.744
How?

00:01:58.744 --> 00:02:00.704
- Well, I don't know how.

00:02:00.704 --> 00:02:03.624
I mean,
you're the incident commander.

00:02:03.624 --> 00:02:05.334
- Not only are you a reporter

00:02:05.334 --> 00:02:07.836
for "The New York Times"
and an incredible journalist,

00:02:07.836 --> 00:02:09.379
but you're also
a medical doctor.

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:13.342
- I actually did an MD
and a PhD,

00:02:13.342 --> 00:02:17.554
a combined program, and the idea
was to be a clinician...

00:02:17.554 --> 00:02:18.889
- Mm-hmm.
- ...practicing medicine,

00:02:18.889 --> 00:02:20.891
and also do research
that related to that topic

00:02:20.891 --> 00:02:24.478
and try to, you know,
do independent investigating.

00:02:24.478 --> 00:02:26.605
It all kind of started
with a year off,

00:02:26.605 --> 00:02:28.607
or what I envisioned
to be a year off

00:02:28.607 --> 00:02:30.067
between med school
and residency,

00:02:30.067 --> 00:02:33.779
and then my life went
in a very different direction.

00:02:33.779 --> 00:02:36.323
- You went to Bosnia, right?

00:02:36.323 --> 00:02:38.408
- I ended up getting sent
to the border

00:02:38.408 --> 00:02:40.285
of Kosovo and Macedonia

00:02:40.285 --> 00:02:43.330
when refugees were fleeing
across that border,

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:46.208
and I found myself as a--
as a novice doctor

00:02:46.208 --> 00:02:48.961
being put in charge of triage

00:02:48.961 --> 00:02:53.549
as medics or just people brought

00:02:53.549 --> 00:02:56.343
people with various crises
to this tent--

00:02:56.343 --> 00:03:00.639
this small tent
that was serving 100,000 people.

00:03:03.934 --> 00:03:07.354
- So that point in your life
where you're having

00:03:07.354 --> 00:03:09.857
to make decisions about who gets
medical care and who doesn't,

00:03:09.857 --> 00:03:11.483
I mean, that seems
very connected

00:03:11.483 --> 00:03:14.778
to the story
of "Five Days at Memorial."

00:03:14.778 --> 00:03:16.363
How did you first discover
this story of--

00:03:16.363 --> 00:03:19.658
of what happened
at Memorial Hospital?

00:03:19.658 --> 00:03:21.910
- I happened to be volunteering
in New Orleans

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:23.871
after Hurricane Katrina,
and first I went

00:03:23.871 --> 00:03:26.665
with a public health team,
and we worked in the shelters

00:03:26.665 --> 00:03:31.295
where the individuals who had
been displaced from their homes.

00:03:31.295 --> 00:03:33.380
There were still people packed
into stadiums,

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:36.258
and we were doing
some public health research,

00:03:36.258 --> 00:03:39.136
looking at whether people
had medical issues

00:03:39.136 --> 00:03:42.097
in these vast shelters
that needed attention,

00:03:42.097 --> 00:03:43.307
and we found a lot of them

00:03:43.307 --> 00:03:45.184
and information
for future responses.

00:03:45.184 --> 00:03:51.857
And then I think I started
hearing about this rumor

00:03:51.857 --> 00:03:54.860
that these unusual deaths
had occurred at Memorial.

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:58.614
Just like anybody else,
I'm sure I heard it on the news.

00:03:58.614 --> 00:04:01.074
I had just written this book
about a Bosnian war hospital

00:04:01.074 --> 00:04:03.619
that was under siege
for two years,

00:04:03.619 --> 00:04:06.413
and never had I heard
of doctors, nurses

00:04:06.413 --> 00:04:09.458
getting so desperate
that they even talked

00:04:09.458 --> 00:04:12.252
about ending patients' lives.

00:04:12.252 --> 00:04:15.130
- So there's the chapel
where they put all the bodies.

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:17.674
Whoa. What's...

00:04:21.345 --> 00:04:23.013
- The chapel.

00:04:25.307 --> 00:04:27.726
It's just very, very real
to life,

00:04:27.726 --> 00:04:32.314
and this chapel was, um, a site

00:04:32.314 --> 00:04:35.234
where the bodies were placed
and blessed,

00:04:35.234 --> 00:04:40.155
and it's really something
to see it in this way.

00:04:40.155 --> 00:04:41.490
- Yeah.

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:43.116
- I think it will have
quite an impact

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:44.535
when people watch the show.

00:04:44.535 --> 00:04:48.163
The reason that I wanted to look
at the individual actions

00:04:48.163 --> 00:04:51.375
is that I had seen,
as a responder myself

00:04:51.375 --> 00:04:54.837
in disaster
and conflict situations that

00:04:54.837 --> 00:04:56.505
very often our infrastructure
doesn't hold up,

00:04:56.505 --> 00:04:59.967
very often, we individuals,
you, me,

00:04:59.967 --> 00:05:02.010
might be left in a situation

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:04.012
where our ability to act,
our decisions,

00:05:04.012 --> 00:05:06.723
could have
life and death consequences

00:05:06.723 --> 00:05:11.478
because rarely, rarely does
a response go as one would hope.

00:05:11.478 --> 00:05:13.188
- I think one of the things
that you did in the book

00:05:13.188 --> 00:05:15.983
that John and I, you know,
instantly latched on to

00:05:15.983 --> 00:05:17.192
was that it was nonjudgmental,

00:05:17.192 --> 00:05:20.696
that it was really
a presentation

00:05:20.696 --> 00:05:22.447
of all sides of the story.

00:05:22.447 --> 00:05:25.325
- I start letting everybody
in your unit know

00:05:25.325 --> 00:05:27.411
that there aren't any more
National Guard trucks coming,

00:05:27.411 --> 00:05:29.538
and they're pulling the rest
of their people out.

00:05:29.538 --> 00:05:30.998
- What about our people?

00:05:30.998 --> 00:05:32.457
- You are seeing these events
from multiple perspectives

00:05:32.457 --> 00:05:37.087
who are very valid because
depending on where you are

00:05:37.087 --> 00:05:40.382
in this massive
2-city block hospital,

00:05:40.382 --> 00:05:41.800
you are having
a different experience,

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:43.427
so there are many truths,
actually,

00:05:43.427 --> 00:05:46.263
many, many realities,
and it felt really important

00:05:46.263 --> 00:05:47.890
to portray those.

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:50.475
- We're running
out of food and water.

00:05:50.475 --> 00:05:52.936
There's the constant sound
of misery.

00:05:52.936 --> 00:05:55.063
We can't take it much longer.

00:05:55.063 --> 00:05:57.482
- When I wrote this,
and very much the same

00:05:57.482 --> 00:06:01.862
as when you and John wrote,
is that we want people

00:06:01.862 --> 00:06:05.532
to experience this or to--
to go along with it

00:06:05.532 --> 00:06:08.285
as it happened in time,
not just to look back and judge

00:06:08.285 --> 00:06:11.413
but to try to march through
moment by moment

00:06:11.413 --> 00:06:14.166
along with people who didn't
have perfect information,

00:06:14.166 --> 00:06:17.002
who were basically

00:06:17.002 --> 00:06:19.046
dealing with the situation
as it unfolded

00:06:19.046 --> 00:06:20.839
and to have that empathy
for them

00:06:20.839 --> 00:06:24.426
and maybe understanding of--
of what led to

00:06:24.426 --> 00:06:26.386
the events that occurred
that were so shocking.

00:06:26.386 --> 00:06:28.430
- Really,
right from the very beginning

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:30.307
as we were talking about
how to adapt the book,

00:06:30.307 --> 00:06:34.478
we wanted to try to advocate
for all of our characters,

00:06:34.478 --> 00:06:36.146
and we really wanted to feel

00:06:36.146 --> 00:06:38.273
like every character
in the story

00:06:38.273 --> 00:06:39.691
believed in what
they were doing.

00:06:39.691 --> 00:06:41.860
- And action.

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:44.112
- We're getting out
all the patients that we can.

00:06:44.112 --> 00:06:46.865
- Our hope is, like, that we'd
be left to debate and discuss

00:06:46.865 --> 00:06:49.076
some of the ethics
and issues involved

00:06:49.076 --> 00:06:50.786
and really hope that this would
provoke conversations

00:06:50.786 --> 00:06:54.414
after people watched it about
some of the questions raised.

00:06:54.414 --> 00:06:56.416
&lt;i&gt;- I've tried to do good.&lt;/i&gt;

00:06:56.416 --> 00:06:58.335
- Is this person guilty?
Is this person not guilty?

00:06:58.335 --> 00:06:59.837
- Jesus.

00:06:59.837 --> 00:07:01.505
- Should this person
have gone to jail?

00:07:01.505 --> 00:07:03.924
- I need to go back upstairs.
I need to check on a patient.

00:07:03.924 --> 00:07:05.551
- Anything you need to know,

00:07:05.551 --> 00:07:07.010
you can email
our corporate office.

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:08.637
- Why? Your office is--
we're a different hospital.

00:07:08.637 --> 00:07:13.892
- We're evacuating.
You need to go. Now.

00:07:13.892 --> 00:07:16.270
- Was it understandable
and justifiable what they did?

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:19.815
Is it even possible for us
to pass judgment

00:07:19.815 --> 00:07:22.901
on these characters
from the comfort of hindsight?

00:07:24.403 --> 00:07:29.074
- I think it's absolutely fine
that individuals will watch this

00:07:29.074 --> 00:07:31.243
and come away
with different conclusions

00:07:31.243 --> 00:07:35.789
about what they feel,
you know, should have happened,

00:07:35.789 --> 00:07:38.083
but what I really want
is for them to understand

00:07:38.083 --> 00:07:39.751
what &lt;i&gt;did&lt;/i&gt; happen,
to have the facts--

00:07:39.751 --> 00:07:42.045
that's very material,

00:07:42.045 --> 00:07:44.214
and--and that helps us
understand

00:07:44.214 --> 00:07:49.052
and make informed kind of
judgments in our own mind.

00:07:49.052 --> 00:07:51.847
I think as human beings,
we have an aversion

00:07:51.847 --> 00:07:53.807
to, you know, really embracing
the possibility

00:07:53.807 --> 00:07:57.102
that the worst case scenario
will happen.

00:07:57.102 --> 00:07:58.478
It's, like, natural.

00:07:58.478 --> 00:08:00.689
We don't wanna go there,
but also, more broadly,

00:08:00.689 --> 00:08:03.859
to invest in things
that are rare but foreseeable

00:08:03.859 --> 00:08:07.029
as potential threats,
like a flood in New Orleans...

00:08:07.029 --> 00:08:10.157
- Yeah.
- ...and could be catastrophic.

00:08:10.157 --> 00:08:11.617
But what I think
we can do validly,

00:08:11.617 --> 00:08:15.579
and--and all of us who--
who watch this or read the book

00:08:15.579 --> 00:08:17.122
can do and should do
is think not,

00:08:17.122 --> 00:08:20.042
like, "What would I have done?"
because we can't know that,

00:08:20.042 --> 00:08:21.710
but what would I want to do?

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:24.588
- This is why this is such
a timely story,

00:08:24.588 --> 00:08:26.381
'cause it's not just about
this little piece of history.

00:08:26.381 --> 00:08:30.260
I think it speaks to something
so much larger.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:32.971
I hope that they will empathize
with the people

00:08:32.971 --> 00:08:36.308
who actually went through this,
and I hope that people will

00:08:36.308 --> 00:08:40.354
come away with an understanding
of why it is important

00:08:40.354 --> 00:08:42.856
that we think about crises
before they happen.

00:08:42.856 --> 00:08:43.941
- We all know that now...
- Yeah.

00:08:43.941 --> 00:08:45.359
...with COVID, but that takes

00:08:45.359 --> 00:08:47.486
some investment
on a personal level.

00:08:47.486 --> 00:08:49.571
- And you're just looking
at one hospital

00:08:49.571 --> 00:08:51.448
in the middle of this
whole hurricane and flooding

00:08:51.448 --> 00:08:52.908
and all the situation.

00:08:52.908 --> 00:08:55.661
It's so metaphoric for
all the things that went on

00:08:55.661 --> 00:08:57.871
in the city, on the state level,
on the federal level,

00:08:57.871 --> 00:09:00.499
and people who were displaced
were often

00:09:00.499 --> 00:09:02.084
the most disadvantaged.

00:09:02.084 --> 00:09:04.962
- And, really, my passion
for telling stories like this

00:09:04.962 --> 00:09:08.173
is because these inequities,
these structural issues

00:09:08.173 --> 00:09:09.466
are with us all the time.

00:09:09.466 --> 00:09:12.636
When do we stand
and pay attention to them?

00:09:12.636 --> 00:09:14.555
We need to learn
from our history, right?

00:09:14.555 --> 00:09:16.139
That's the purpose.

00:09:16.139 --> 00:09:17.891
Our hope is that we'd be left
to debate and discuss

00:09:17.891 --> 00:09:19.560
some of the ethics and issues

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:22.563
and some
of the questions raised.

